<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sino-Vietnamese War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sino-Vietnamese War was a brief war fought in February and March 1979, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in response to Vietnam's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and its alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Chinese forces captured several provincial capitals, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a>, then withdrew after about a month, both sides claiming victory. Border clashes continued through the 1980s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sino-vietnamese-war.jpg" alt="Sino-Vietnamese War" width="320" height="289" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vietnam_china.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> had a long history, and communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> aided the Viet Minh against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After Stalin's death in 1953 relations between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and China deteriorated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> did not support China against the Soviets. China viewed the growing Soviet-Vietnamese relationship with alarm and feared encirclement. Tensions by the mid-1970s also involved Vietnam's treatment of ethnic Chinese, border conflicts and Vietnam's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> supported the Maoist Khmer Rouge, who came to power under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a> in 1975. After numerous border clashes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> on 25 December 1978, and by 7 January 1979 its forces had entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>. Border skirmishes rose from 125 in 1974 to 2,175 in 1978.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 17 February 1979 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> force of about 200,000 troops with about 200 tanks entered northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, in the army's first major combat operation since the China-India War of 1962. It advanced in two directions, the eastern commanded by Xu Shiyou towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-bang">Cao Bằng</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a> and Quảng Ninh, and the western commanded by Yang Dezhi towards Ha Tuyen, Hoang Lien Son and Lai Châu. Chinese forces quickly captured several cities near the border.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> took no direct military action but supplied intelligence and equipment and airlifted Vietnamese troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> to the north. It also deployed troops on the Sino-Soviet and Mongolian-Chinese borders, though it refused to defend its ally directly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> had to keep most of its forces along its northern frontier as a deterrent. On 6 March China declared its punitive mission accomplished and withdrew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Casualty figures are disputed. A Vietnamese agency claimed over 44,000 Chinese casualties, which Western analysts thought greatly inflated, and leaks from Chinese military sources indicate 6,954 Chinese dead. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> held 1,636 Vietnamese prisoners and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> 238 Chinese, and they were exchanged in May and June 1979. Vietnam continued to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> until 1989, so China did not achieve one of its stated aims, though it showed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> could not protect its Vietnamese ally.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> the authorities expelled about 8,000 Hoa people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> to southern New Economic Zones in spring 1979 and expelled 20,468 members from the Communist Party. Border skirmishes continued through the 1980s, including a naval battle over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spratly-islands">Spratly Islands</a> in 1988. Relations were normalised in November 1991, and a border pact was signed in 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sino-Vietnamese War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>China, Democratic Kampuchea’s patron, retaliated in February 1979 with the <b>Sino-Vietnamese War</b>, sending a large force across the northern border that took Lang Son and other provincial towns before withdrawing in March after heavy losses on both sides.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li></ul></section>
